  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe upper-level trough responsible for the cooler airmass over\nthe region will gradually exit east into New England today as a\nlarge upper ridge begins to expand east into the Midwest and\nGreat Lakes into Saturday. Mainly dry and seasonable weather\nwill continue as surface high pressure remains across the area.\nA few showers or storms are possible across far Northeast Ohio\nand Northwest Pennsylvania Saturday afternoon as a weak\nshortwave moves east through the Lower Great Lakes, aided by a\ndeveloping lake breeze. \n\nTemperatures will gradually warm in the mid to upper 70s today,\nincreasing into the low to mid-80s by Saturday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nConfidence is increasing for a few stronger storms beginning\nlate Sunday into Tuesday as several potent shortwaves traverse \nthe base of a strong upper-level trough across Hudson Bay. \n\nThere will be a couple of periods to watch for developing \nstorms, the first of which appears to be Sunday night into \nMonday as a leading shortwave enters the Lower Great Lakes, \nthough confidence is a bit lower given the more mesoscale \ndynamics at play. Slightly higher confidence exists for a more \nwidespread strong thunderstorm potential in the Monday night \ninto Tuesday timeframe with convection supported by the passage \nof a potent mid and upper-level trough. Given the favorable \nnorthwesterly shear and anticipated linear storm mode, if any \nstorms were to become strong to severe, damaging wind gusts \nappear to be the primary hazard. Will continue to monitor the \nthreat over the next couple of forecast iterations.",
